# Coding conventions

Cursor doesn't read `CLAUDE.md` automatically, so cross-cutting conventions that matter to both agent types are mirrored here. `CLAUDE.md` → **Key conventions** is the canonical, fuller list — check it too.

## Labels only — no explanatory copy on screen

Every surface in this repo — the site and the dev layer alike — ships with **labels only**. A control names itself and stops there. It has to be obvious from **how it looks and how it behaves**, not from prose sitting next to it explaining what it is for.

**Do not put on screen:** sub-titles that restate the heading above them; helper or "meta" text under a field, toggle, or panel ("Choose an option below", "This controls the grid gap"); *what this panel does* intros; parenthetical hints; onboarding blurbs; explanatory paragraphs in empty states; tooltips written as an explanation rather than a name.

**Still fine:** the label itself; a unit or format shown inline (`px`, `%`) where it is part of the value, not commentary; error text that says what broke and what to do next; the site's actual editorial content (About, case studies) — this rule governs **chrome**, not copy; and **accessible names** (`aria-label`, `title` on an icon-only control), which are invisible to sighted users and mandatory for everyone else. Never strip an accessible name to satisfy this rule.

The explanation goes in a **source comment**, `CLAUDE.md`, or `docs/` — explaining in the source is free, explaining on screen is paid for by every reader afterwards.

**The test:** if a control needs a sentence, the control is wrong. Fix the affordance — truer label, clearer grouping, more obvious interaction — rather than annotating a weak one. The dev layer's dashed hairline is the model: it is legible because a dashed line *means* editable, not because a caption says so.

Canonical personal standard: Atlas `standards.md` → *Design — Labels only in a UI*. Also mirrored in `CLAUDE.md` → **Key conventions**.

## Viewport height units

Never use raw `svh` (or `vh`) for a height constraint that matters visually — Microsoft Edge on iOS doesn't reliably honor `svh`, letting it track the address bar's show/hide mid-scroll and shove blocks below it up/down. Use the shared helper `src/lib/safeVh.ts` (`bindSafeVh()` in `Layout.astro`), which sets `--vh-safe` once on load and only recomputes on a genuine width change (never on the height-only resize an iOS toolbar toggle fires). Keep `svh` only as the pre-JS/no-JS fallback: `calc(var(--vh-safe, 1svh) * N)`. Do not re-implement this per component. See issues #96 / #97.

## Appear / disappear motion

Dev chrome (and similar UI) should not pop in or out:
- **Standalone panels** (e.g. `DevOverlay` tuners) — fade in with a slight rise; mirror on exit. See `DevOverlay.astro`.
- **Covering veils** over an existing component (e.g. cancel / status overlays) — fade only, no rise.

## `prefers-reduced-motion` — suppress movement, not fades

**Reduced motion means "don't move things", not "don't animate".** A fade keeps its easing; only travel and resize are removed. The reference is iOS itself: with Reduce Motion on, switching apps still animates — it crossfades instead of sliding. Users who set the preference are avoiding vestibular triggers, not asking for a UI that snaps.

Apply it per property, not per component:

| Animating | Under reduced motion |
| --- | --- |
| `opacity` | **Keep the transition unchanged.** |
| `transform: translate` / a rise | Drop the movement, keep the fade — set the offset to `0`, not the duration. |
| `transform: scale`, `width`/`height`, layout shift | Suppress the transition; let it settle instantly, or crossfade between states. |

**Do not reach for `transition: none` on a rule that only fades**, and do not zero a shared duration variable that a fade also reads — that is how you remove the one kind of motion that was safe to keep.

**This applies to JS timing too, and that is where it usually hides.** A `prefersReducedMotion()` check that returns a `0` duration, skips the enter/exit class dance, or calls the "finish" path immediately makes the UI snap no matter how correct the CSS is. Three separate bugs in `#205` were exactly this shape — `chromeFadeMs()` returning `0` in `DevModeController.astro`, and `DevOverlay.astro` setting `hidden` in the same frame so the panel vanished rather than faded. Grep for `prefers-reduced-motion` in **script** blocks as well as styles.

Genuine movement still gets suppressed — the section-lift `scale(1.02)`, the drop-indicator height animation, and the crop-stage transforms all keep their overrides.
